#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""cc-stack · cc-state — the single reader/writer of the stack's state stores.

Phase C (docs/state-model.md §7): the backend is ONE sqlite library in WAL mode. The
four TSVs are imported on first open and renamed aside, never deleted. What the facade
already gave — one implementation of every field access, in place of 46 awk parse
points spread over cc-hooks.sh / cc-board.sh / cc-dispatch.sh / worktree.zsh — the
engine adds to: the nine mkdir-lock copies are gone entirely (WAL gives multi-reader /
single-writer for free) and every write is a transaction instead of a write-tmp-then-
rename, so the half-applied archive move cannot happen at all.

Verb contract (spec §4): each subcommand answers ONE caller question; output is TSV
with no header, stdout only; errors go to stderr. rc: 0 = success (including the
deliberate silent no-ops that mirror today's callers), 1 = a query that found
nothing (task-get / tab-owner / tab-resolve / task-opened-recently / exists), 2 =
usage error. Safe to call from sh: no verb reads stdin unless explicitly pointed at
it (`load <store> -`), no output on failure paths that today are silent.

Stores, and the legacy TSV each one was imported FROM (phase D retired the four
per-store overrides: the library's location is the only knob, and the legacy files are
found beside it — see _p):
  tasks   worktree-tasks.tsv           time\\tbranch\\tsurface\\tdir\\tcaller\\ttask\\tparent\\tlargs
  status  worktree-status.tsv          dir\\tstate\\tunix-ts
  archive worktree-tasks-archive.tsv   a tasks row verbatim + \\tmerged-at[ + \\tmerged-into]
  tabs    opened-tabs.tsv              suuid\\towner\\tdir\\tsession\\ttime

Phase D (model convergence) closed the last three gaps the Phase A API was written
against: state/state_ts and the tab-opened dedup timestamp are columns on the task
row (no status sidecar, no $TMPDIR marker), and task-archive now PERSISTS its
<merged-into>. task-mark-opened is still deliberately NOT part of task-add: the
stamp may only be set once the tab actually OPENED — a failed dispatch must leave no
stamp, or it silently eats the retry for 120s (cc-dispatch.sh's "Only CHECK here;
write the stamp after success").

Row discipline (the TAB-collapse class, cc-board.sh's access note): rewrites select
whole lines, modify only the fields they mean to touch, and re-emit the rest by
lossless split/join — `"\t".join(line.split("\t"))` with untouched fields is
byte-identical by construction (a split can never lose a field or a TAB, unlike
re-printfing parsed VARIABLES, which is what fossilized the shift class). A 7-field
legacy row round-trips as 7 fields; an empty middle field stays empty; a rewrite
whose row set comes up empty DELETES the file — the behaviour of every one of
today's rewriters.

Dir matching — ONE rule for every verb (the round-2 gate's B10): a stored row
matches a caller's <dir> when the stored string IS the given string, or when it
canonicalizes (while it still exists) to the canonical form of the given string.
task-add additionally canonicalizes what it WRITES (spec §3.5 defect 3's fix
direction: normalization moves to the write side), so new rows are always physical
while legacy logical rows (/var vs /private/var) still resolve on read.

Intentional deviations from today's byte stream, both gate-ruled (spec §3.5 #7/#8):
  · task summaries truncate by CHARACTER, not by BYTE — today's `cut -c1-140`
    under LC_ALL=C slices multibyte summaries mid-character and writes invalid
    UTF-8 to disk; the facade does not replicate that bug.
  · an unborn branch's `git rev-parse --abbrev-ref HEAD` exits 128 after printing
    "HEAD", which today's `$(… || echo '?')` captures as a two-line "HEAD\\n?"
    branch field, corrupting the row; _git_branch returns a clean '?'.
"""
import os
import sqlite3
import subprocess
import sys
import time

# Non-UTF-8 bytes ride through argv and the files as lone surrogates
# (errors="surrogateescape" below); stdout/stderr must pass them back out the same
# way — a bare print() would raise UnicodeEncodeError and take the whole board down
# over one stray byte that awk never even notices (round-2 gate B2).
for _s in (sys.stdout, sys.stderr):
    try:
        _s.reconfigure(errors="surrogateescape")
    except (AttributeError, OSError):
        pass

# ── legacy store paths ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Phase D, spec §8 invariant 9: the four CC_*_FILE overrides are RETIRED. A legacy TSV
# is now found beside the library, full stop — which is what the store set has always
# physically been, and what _migratable already had to re-assert as a RULE because the
# five paths could be pointed at each other's directories independently. That hole was
# not hypothetical: one shell command that set CC_STATE_DB and nothing else imported —
# and renamed away — the live ledgers into a scratch library it then threw away.
# Deriving the paths makes the same accident unrepresentable instead of merely checked,
# and collapses the sandbox to ONE variable, which is the actual invariant ("tests never
# write real state" had been failing on half-set overrides, not on a missing check).
def _p(name):
    d = os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(_db_path()))
    return os.path.join(d or ".", name)

PATHS = {
    "tasks":   lambda: _p("worktree-tasks.tsv"),
    "status":  lambda: _p("worktree-status.tsv"),
    "archive": lambda: _p("worktree-tasks-archive.tsv"),
    "tabs":    lambda: _p("opened-tabs.tsv"),
}

# The retired names, and which store each one used to point at. Kept ONLY to say so out
# loud: someone whose shell still exports one would otherwise have their rows go quietly
# invisible, which is the exact failure shape this campaign keeps hunting.
RETIRED = (("CC_TASKS_FILE", "tasks"), ("CC_STATUS_FILE", "status"),
           ("CC_ARCHIVE_FILE", "archive"), ("CC_TABS_FILE", "tabs"))

def _open_rd(p):
    """A caller-supplied file, NOT a store: tab-prune takes the raw cmux live map as an
    argument and has to read it the same lossless way the stores used to be read."""
    return open(p, encoding="utf-8", errors="surrogateescape", newline="")

# ── the library ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# One sqlite file in WAL mode replaces the four TSVs and, with them, the mkdir lock:
# WAL gives multi-reader/single-writer for free, and a write is a transaction instead
# of a write-tmp-then-rename. CC_STATE_DB is its own override and is NOT derived from
# one of the four legacy paths — deriving it would put the library wherever whichever
# variable happened to be set (spec 8 invariant 9). The legacy vars stay meaningful:
# they are where the MIGRATION looks for data to import.
#
# python 3.9 is the floor (the hook resolves to /usr/bin/python3 = 3.9.6 under a
# stripped PATH), so: no connect(autocommit=) — isolation_level=None plus an explicit
# BEGIN IMMEDIATE — no match statement, no runtime-evaluated X | Y annotation.
def _db_path():
    return os.environ.get("CC_STATE_DB") or os.path.join(
        os.path.expanduser("~"), ".config", "cc-stack", "cc-state.db")

# The columns each store's row is spread over, BY NAME (§3.1). D2: they used to be
# f1..fN, which made every verb take fields by POSITION — the same shape as the awk
# parsing whose field-shift bugs started this whole campaign.
#
# What did NOT go away with the names: `nf` and `fx`. The D plan's §1 assumed naming
# forces dropping them and priced two "costs" on that assumption; both were wrong. They
# are round-trip MACHINERY, not semantics, and they are orthogonal to what the columns
# are called — so `dump` stays byte-exact and the C-phase pins hold. See _to_row.
#
# `archive` is a tasks row plus two: merged_at, and merged_into which until D2 rode in
# `fx` (it was appended to the LINE, which is what the board and gwt-log read). Naming
# it promotes the same fact to a column without changing a byte of dump's output.
# `tasks` deliberately does NOT carry merged_at/merged_into: §3.1's own trade-off moves
# an archived row OUT to the archive table, so a live task row can never have them.
NAMES = {
    "tasks":   ("created_at", "branch", "surface_ref", "dir",
                "caller_ref", "task", "parent", "launch_args"),
    "archive": ("created_at", "branch", "surface_ref", "dir",
                "caller_ref", "task", "parent", "launch_args", "merged_at", "merged_into"),
    "tabs":    ("suuid", "owner", "dir", "session", "ts"),
}
COLS = dict((store, len(names)) for store, names in NAMES.items())
COLS["status"] = 3          # a projection, not a table — see _get_status
# The v1/v2 widths, needed only by the one-way rebuild in _upgrade.
OLD_COLS = {"tasks": 8, "archive": 9, "tabs": 5}

class _Ix(object):
    """Field positions BY NAME, derived from the column names above.

    The verbs still work on whole TSV lines — a rewrite has to re-emit the fields it
    did not mean to touch byte-for-byte, and that is what a line gives you and a
    reconstructed row does not. What they stopped doing is spelling the position: a
    hand-written index and the `dir` column could drift apart, `f[TASK.dir]` and the
    column cannot, because there is now exactly ONE definition of the row's shape.
    §39 pins that no store row is indexed by a bare number again — which is why this
    note says "a hand-written index" instead of showing you one."""
    def __init__(self, names):
        for i, n in enumerate(names):
            setattr(self, n, i)

TASK = _Ix(NAMES["tasks"])
TAB = _Ix(NAMES["tabs"])
# The sidecar projection has no table of its own (it IS two columns on the task row),
# but `dump status` / `load status` / task-set-state still speak its 3-field line.
SIDE = _Ix(("dir", "state", "ts"))

# D 期（模型收敛）：真正的表只剩三张。`status` 仍是一个 STORE —— 动词、dump / load、沙箱
# 变量全部照旧 —— 但它不再有自己的表，而是 tasks 上两列的投影（§3.1「sidecar 只是任务行的
# 两列」）。虚拟化整个发生在 _get / _put 里，上面 20 个动词一行没动，与 C 期换引擎用的是
# 同一道缝。副作用是一条不变量被结构化了：状态行只能属于一个已存在的任务行，孤儿 sidecar
# 从此无处可存（今天 task-prune 本来就会扫掉它们）。
TABLES = ("tasks", "archive", "tabs")
SCHEMA_VERSION = 3
# tasks 上不属于 TSV 行的列。前两列吃掉 worktree-status.tsv；tab_opened_ts 吃掉 $TMPDIR
# 去重标记。全部可空 —— 一行没有状态、没开过 tab，都是常态而不是缺省值。
# （D 期一度还在这里放了 merged_at / merged_into。D2 把它们移到 archive 表上：§3.1 的取舍
# 段说归档行写入时移出到 archive，所以一条活着的任务行永远不可能带这两个值。）
STATE_COLS = (("state", "BLOB"), ("state_ts", "BLOB"), ("tab_opened_ts", "INTEGER"))
# 导入顺序：tasks 必须在 status 之前 —— 状态要落到任务行上，任务行还不存在就全丢了。
# 字典序恰好把 status 排在 tasks 前面,所以这个顺序不能靠排序得出（变异确认：换成字典序
# 打红 3 条）。
MIGRATE_ORDER = ("tasks", "archive", "tabs", "status")

# Three things the row↔column round trip needs, none of them optional:
#   · BLOB, not TEXT. read_lines has always used errors="surrogateescape" so a non-UTF-8
#     byte rides through the way awk passes it; a TEXT column cannot hold the lone
#     surrogate that produces — sqlite3 raises UnicodeEncodeError on the INSERT. BLOB +
#     encode/decode("utf-8","surrogateescape") round-trips it losslessly. Measured, not
#     assumed: the TEXT path fails on the first row that carries one.
#   · nf. tasks holds 7-field legacy rows next to 8-field live ones (archive 8 and 9).
#     Rendering from fixed columns would pad a 7-field row to 8 and change dump's bytes,
#     so the row's ORIGINAL field count is stored and the render truncates to it.
#   · fx. A row wider than its table's columns can only come from a hand edit or `load`,
#     but silently dropping its tail would be data loss in the one direction the human
#     cannot undo — the overflow rides in fx so dump gives back exactly what went in.
# Row ORDER is the fourth: a TSV is insertion-ordered and sqlite has no implicit order,
# so every table carries a monotonic seq and every read is ORDER BY seq. task-prune
# --compact's newest-per-dir depends on it; this is not an optimization.
def _cols(store):
    return ", ".join(NAMES[store])

def _create(conn):
    for store in TABLES:
        conn.execute("CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS %s (seq INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, "
                     "nf INTEGER NOT NULL, %s, fx BLOB%s)"
                     % (store, ", ".join("%s BLOB" % c for c in NAMES[store]),
                        "".join(", %s %s" % (c, t) for c, t in STATE_COLS)
                        if store == "tasks" else ""))

def _schema(conn):
    _create(conn)
    _upgrade(conn)

def _rebuild_named(conn, have):
    """v1/v2 的 `f1..fN` → v3 的具名列。

    重建而不是 `RENAME COLUMN`，因为形状本身也变了：`archive` 多一列（`merged_into`，
    此前作为第 10 个字段寄居在 `fx` 里），`tasks` 少两列（`merged_at`/`merged_into`，在
    那张表上永远是 NULL）。整个搬运走 `_to_line` → `_to_row`，也就是说「第 10 个字段从
    `fx` 升格成列」是宽度变化的**自然结果**，没有一处手写的字段搬移——手写的那种正是
    这场战役一直在消灭的东西。"""
    st = [c for c, _ in STATE_COLS if c in have]     # v2 有这些列，v1 没有
    carried = []
    if st:
        for row in conn.execute("SELECT f4, %s FROM tasks ORDER BY seq" % ", ".join(st)):
            vals = dict(zip(st, row[1:]))
            if any(v is not None for v in vals.values()):
                carried.append(("" if row[0] is None else _dec(row[0]),
                                [vals.get(c) for c, _ in STATE_COLS]))
    data = {}
    for store in TABLES:
        old_n = OLD_COLS[store]
        data[store] = [_to_line(r, old_n) for r in conn.execute(
            "SELECT nf, %s, fx FROM %s ORDER BY seq"
            % (", ".join("f%d" % (i + 1) for i in range(old_n)), store))]
    for store in TABLES:
        conn.execute("DROP TABLE %s" % store)
    _create(conn)
    for store in TABLES:
        n = COLS[store]
        conn.executemany("INSERT INTO %s (nf, %s, fx) VALUES (%s)"
                         % (store, _cols(store), ", ".join("?" * (n + 2))),
                         [_to_row(l, n) for l in data[store]])
    _restore(conn, carried)

def _upgrade(conn):
    """v1（四张表）/ v2（三张表 + 位置列）→ v3（具名列）。库内迁移，不碰磁盘上任何文件。

    幂等靠两个独立的问题，不靠版本号一个：`tasks` 到底长什么样（有 `f1` 就是旧形状），
    以及 `status` 表在不在。一个被打断的升级下次接着做，而不是在已经改过的表上重来。
    整个过程一个事务，失败则库停在原版本、按原路径继续跑（沿用 C 期的降级契约）。"""
    if conn.execute("PRAGMA user_version").fetchone()[0] >= SCHEMA_VERSION:
        return
    have = set(r[1] for r in conn.execute("PRAGMA table_info(tasks)"))
    legacy = "f1" in have
    sidecar = conn.execute("SELECT count(*) FROM sqlite_master "
                           "WHERE type='table' AND name='status'").fetchone()[0]
    conn.execute("BEGIN IMMEDIATE")
    try:
        if legacy:
            _rebuild_named(conn, have)
        if sidecar:
            rows = [_to_line(r, 3) for r in conn.execute(
                "SELECT nf, f1, f2, f3, fx FROM status ORDER BY seq")]
            # ONLY when the table actually holds rows. _put_status clears every carried
            # state before re-applying, so folding an EMPTY sidecar would wipe state that
            # is already in the columns — which is exactly the new→old→new round trip: old
            # code re-creates an empty `status` and stamps the version back, leaving the
            # real state sitting untouched in the columns it cannot see. Downgrading must
            # cost visibility, never data.
            if rows:
                _put_status(conn, rows)
            conn.execute("DROP TABLE status")
        conn.execute("PRAGMA user_version=%d" % SCHEMA_VERSION)
        conn.execute("COMMIT")
    except BaseException:
        try:
            conn.execute("ROLLBACK")
        except sqlite3.Error:
            pass
        raise

def _enc(s):
    return s.encode("utf-8", "surrogateescape")

def _dec(b):
    return b.decode("utf-8", "surrogateescape")

def _to_row(line, n):
    f = line.split("\t")
    head = [_enc(x) for x in f[:n]]
    head += [None] * (n - len(head))
    tail = _enc("\t".join(f[n:])) if len(f) > n else None
    return [len(f)] + head + [tail]

def _to_line(row, n):
    nf, vals, fx = row[0], row[1:1 + n], row[1 + n]
    out = [("" if v is None else _dec(v)) for v in vals[:min(nf, n)]]
    if fx is not None:
        out.extend(_dec(fx).split("\t"))
    return "\t".join(out)

_CONN = None
_LAST_ERR = None          # why the last _connect gave up; dump is the only loud reader

def _open(p):
    conn = sqlite3.connect(p, timeout=3.0, isolation_level=None)
    conn.execute("PRAGMA journal_mode=WAL")
    conn.execute("PRAGMA busy_timeout=3000")
    return conn

def _connect(create):
    """The library, or None when there is none and none is wanted.

    `create` is load-bearing for COST, not for correctness: a read must not bring the
    file into existence, because cc-hooks.sh's hot path is `[ -f <db> ] || [ -f <tsv> ]`
    and an empty library created by some passing read would arm it forever — two python
    startups on every prompt of every session on a machine that has no state at all
    (0.1: 15 ms each, and the check itself is 0.019 ms). A read still migrates when
    legacy TSVs are there, because that is where the data IS.

    Every failure path returns None instead of raising: the hook contract is a silent
    no-op on a missing python3, a corrupt library, a full disk."""
    global _CONN, _LAST_ERR
    _LAST_ERR = None
    if _CONN is not None:
        return _CONN
    p = _db_path()
    # Before any branch: a read that finds neither library nor importable legacy returns
    # None below, and that early return is exactly the case the notice is FOR.
    _warn_retired()
    try:
        if os.path.exists(p):
            conn = _open(p)
            _schema(conn)
        else:
            # Beside the library, or not part of this store set at all — the rule that
            # used to be _migratable's explicit same-directory check is now the way the
            # paths are BUILT, so existence is the whole question.
            legacy = [s for s in MIGRATE_ORDER if os.path.exists(PATHS[s]())]
            if not legacy and not create:
                return None
            d = os.path.dirname(p)
            if d and not os.path.isdir(d):
                os.makedirs(d, exist_ok=True)
            conn = _open(p)
            try:
                _schema(conn)
                if legacy:
                    _migrate(conn, legacy)
            except BaseException:
                # spec 6: a failed migration creates NO library and renames NOTHING —
                # the machine keeps running the way it did, and the next invocation
                # retries. Half a library would be worse than none: the legacy rows
                # would be both imported and still on disk.
                try:
                    conn.close()
                except sqlite3.Error:
                    pass
                for suffix in ("", "-wal", "-shm"):
                    try:
                        os.unlink(p + suffix)
                    except OSError:
                        pass
                raise
        _CONN = conn
        return conn
    except (sqlite3.Error, OSError, ValueError) as e:
        _LAST_ERR = e
        return None

_WARNED = False

def _warn_retired():
    """The four CC_*_FILE overrides are gone; say so to anyone still exporting one.

    Without this the retirement is a silent behaviour change for the one person it can
    hurt: a shell that still points CC_TASKS_FILE somewhere now gets a library built from
    whatever sits beside CC_STATE_DB instead, and the moved file is never read again. The
    board would just be empty, forever, with nothing to grep for. stderr only, never the
    rc — the hook path redirects and §39 pins that rather than trusting it — and once per
    process, so a board render that makes several calls says it once.
    Note this fires on the VALUE being set, not on the file existing: an override pointing
    at a path that never existed is still someone who believes it works."""
    global _WARNED
    if _WARNED:
        return
    hits = [(env, store) for env, store in RETIRED if os.environ.get(env)]
    if not hits:
        return
    _WARNED = True
    for env, store in hits:
        v = os.environ[env]
        print("cc-state: %s is retired and IGNORED — the store set is now whatever sits "
              "beside CC_STATE_DB. If %s still holds rows: cc-state load %s %s"
              % (env, v, store, v), file=sys.stderr)

def _read_legacy(path):
    """A legacy TSV exactly as read_lines used to read it: no universal-newline
    translation (a stray \\r\\n row must not be rewritten), surrogateescape for the
    non-UTF-8 bytes, and a final line WITHOUT its newline is still a line."""
    with open(path, encoding="utf-8", errors="surrogateescape", newline="") as f:
        rows = f.read().split("\n")
    if rows and rows[-1] == "":
        rows.pop()
    return rows

def _migrate(conn, legacy):
    """Import the legacy TSVs and rename them aside, in ONE transaction.

    The rename happens INSIDE the transaction on purpose: if it fails the import rolls
    back with it, so the rows can never end up both in the library and on disk. Old
    files are renamed, never deleted (spec 9's risk row: a migration that loses the
    board is unrecoverable; a renamed file is not).

    Two processes racing is safe because BEGIN IMMEDIATE serializes them AND the file
    existence is re-checked inside the transaction: the loser finds the winner already
    renamed the TSVs away and imports nothing, instead of importing them twice."""
    renamed = []
    conn.execute("BEGIN IMMEDIATE")
    try:
        ts = str(int(time.time()))
        for store in legacy:
            src = PATHS[store]()
            if not os.path.exists(src):
                continue                     # the other process won the race
            rows = _read_legacy(src)
            _put(conn, store, rows)
            dst = "%s.migrated.%s" % (src, ts)
            k = 0
            while os.path.exists(dst):
                k += 1
                dst = "%s.migrated.%s.%d" % (src, ts, k)
            os.rename(src, dst)
            renamed.append((src, dst))
        conn.execute("COMMIT")
    except BaseException:
        try:
            conn.execute("ROLLBACK")
        except sqlite3.Error:
            pass
        for src, dst in reversed(renamed):
            try:
                os.rename(dst, src)
            except OSError:
                pass
        raise

def _get_status(conn):
    """The sidecar as it always looked: dir\tstate\tunix-ts, one row per dir.

    Two rules the file had and the projection must keep:
      · the KEY IS PHYSICAL. `task-set-state` always wrote the canonical dir and the
        board render joins on that form — while the tasks row keeps whatever string was
        recorded at dispatch, which for a legacy row is the logical /var/... path. So
        the projection canonicalizes on the way out; emitting f4 raw would silently stop
        the board joining exactly the rows defect 3 was fixed for.
        It must be canon_or_raw, NOT canon: canon_or_raw is what dir_match applies to
        the row side, and it deliberately refuses to realpath a path that is not a
        directory. A dead worktree's row would come out of the projection as a realpath
        that dir_match then cannot match back — so every read→write round trip (prune,
        clear-state, the board's prune-on-read) would silently DROP the state of every
        dir that has already been removed. Same function on both sides, or the
        projection is not lossless.
      · ORDER is the tasks row's seq, i.e. dispatch order — the same order the file
        had, because both answer "when did this dir first appear".
    Rows whose state is NULL are simply not sidecar rows."""
    return ["\t".join((canon_or_raw(_dec(d)) if d is not None else "",
                       "" if st is None else _dec(st),
                       "" if ts is None else _dec(ts)))
            for d, st, ts in conn.execute("SELECT dir, state, state_ts FROM tasks "
                                          "WHERE state IS NOT NULL ORDER BY seq")]

def _put_status(conn, lines):
    """Replace the sidecar: clear every carried state, then re-apply row by row.

    Matching goes through dir_match — the ONE row-matching rule — not string equality
    on f4: the caller may name the canonical path while the row holds the logical one
    (or the reverse), and that pair is exactly what defect 3 was about. Among matches
    the LAST (highest seq) wins: that is the row every reader means by "this worktree".

    A row naming a dir with NO tasks row lands nowhere and is dropped — the orphan
    class task-prune already sweeps, now structurally unrepresentable (plan §1.5 A)."""
    rows = [(seq, "" if d is None else _dec(d))
            for seq, d in conn.execute("SELECT seq, dir FROM tasks ORDER BY seq")]
    conn.execute("UPDATE tasks SET state=NULL, state_ts=NULL")
    for line in lines:
        f = line.split("\t")
        raw = f[SIDE.dir] if f else ""
        c = canon(raw)
        hit = None
        for seq, d in rows:
            if dir_match(d, raw, c):
                hit = seq
        if hit is None:
            continue
        conn.execute("UPDATE tasks SET state=?, state_ts=? WHERE seq=?",
                     (_enc(f[SIDE.state]) if len(f) > SIDE.state else None,
                      _enc(f[SIDE.ts]) if len(f) > SIDE.ts else None, hit))

# The columns that do NOT live in the TSV line. Every verb above the storage layer still
# works on whole lines, so a rewrite of `tasks` is DELETE + re-INSERT of the row columns —
# silently took these with it until this pair existed. `task-set-ref <dir> s:9` wiped the
# state of every worktree on the board, and the board just rendered "-". Anything keyed to
# a task row rather than to its text has to be carried across the rewrite by hand.
CARRIED = ("state", "state_ts", "tab_opened_ts")

def _carry(conn):
    """Snapshot the carried columns, keyed by the row's dir. Rows carrying nothing are
    skipped so the common rewrite (no state anywhere) stays free."""
    out = []
    for row in conn.execute("SELECT dir, %s FROM tasks ORDER BY seq" % ", ".join(CARRIED)):
        vals = list(row[1:])
        if any(v is not None for v in vals):
            out.append(("" if row[0] is None else _dec(row[0]), vals))
    return out

def _restore(conn, carried):
    """Re-attach the snapshot after a rewrite, by dir_match — the same rule every verb
    uses. A dir the rewrite DROPPED finds no row and its columns are gone with it, which
    is the point: pruning a task row takes its state with it, no orphan can survive."""
    if not carried:
        return
    rows = [(seq, "" if d is None else _dec(d))
            for seq, d in conn.execute("SELECT seq, dir FROM tasks ORDER BY seq")]
    sets = ", ".join("%s=?" % c for c in CARRIED)
    for raw, vals in carried:
        c = canon(raw)
        hit = None
        for seq, d in rows:
            if dir_match(d, raw, c):
                hit = seq
        if hit is not None:
            conn.execute("UPDATE tasks SET %s WHERE seq=?" % sets, tuple(vals) + (hit,))

def _put(conn, store, lines):
    """Replace a table's rows. seq restarts at 1, so insertion order is file order."""
    if store == "status":
        return _put_status(conn, lines)
    carried = _carry(conn) if store == "tasks" else None
    n = COLS[store]
    conn.execute("DELETE FROM %s" % store)
    if lines:
        conn.executemany(
            "INSERT INTO %s (nf, %s, fx) VALUES (%s)"
            % (store, _cols(store), ", ".join("?" * (n + 2))),
            [_to_row(l, n) for l in lines])
    if carried:
        _restore(conn, carried)

def _get(conn, store):
    if store == "status":
        return _get_status(conn)
    n = COLS[store]
    return [_to_line(r, n) for r in conn.execute(
        "SELECT nf, %s, fx FROM %s ORDER BY seq" % (_cols(store), store))]

# ── the storage layer (the verbs above it still only ever see whole lines) ──────
def read_lines(store):
    """Rows in insertion order. No library and no legacy file → [] (today: awk on a
    missing file prints nothing). Readers take nothing: WAL readers never block a
    writer and are never blocked by one, which is what the mkdir lock was imitating."""
    conn = _connect(False)
    if conn is None:
        return []
    try:
        return _get(conn, store)
    except sqlite3.Error as e:
        globals()["_LAST_ERR"] = e
        return []

def write_lines(store, lines):
    """Replace the store in one transaction. Returns success. An empty row set leaves
    the store with zero rows — the observable end of what used to be `[ -s ] || rm -f`,
    and the reason `exists` was defined on ROWS in Task 1 (there is no per-store file
    left to stat). Writing nothing to a store that does not exist yet does NOT create
    the library: see _connect's note on the hook's hot path."""
    conn = _connect(bool(lines))
    if conn is None:
        return not lines
    try:
        conn.execute("BEGIN IMMEDIATE")
        _put(conn, store, lines)
        conn.execute("COMMIT")
        return True
    except sqlite3.Error:
        try:
            conn.execute("ROLLBACK")
        except sqlite3.Error:
            pass
        return False

def rewrite(store, fn):
    """Read → transform → write inside ONE transaction. Reading the rows into
    write_lines' argument instead would re-introduce the race that lost 18 of 41
    dispatch records under plain campaign concurrency (round-2 gate B1) — the lock
    is gone, the requirement is not. Skips the write when fn returns the rows
    unchanged. fn receives a COPY: an in-place fn then shows up as out != rows and the
    write still fires — without the copy, a mutating fn would return the very object it
    mutated and the skip check would compare an object against itself (round-3 C1)."""
    conn = _connect(False)
    if conn is None:
        out = fn([])                    # an empty store: only an fn that ADDS rows
        if not out:                     # is worth creating the library for
            return True
        conn = _connect(True)
        if conn is None:
            return False
        return write_lines(store, out)
    try:
        conn.execute("BEGIN IMMEDIATE")
        rows = _get(conn, store)
        out = fn(list(rows))
        if out != rows:
            _put(conn, store, out)
        conn.execute("COMMIT")
        return True
    except sqlite3.Error:
        try:
            conn.execute("ROLLBACK")
        except sqlite3.Error:
            pass
        return False
    except BaseException:
        try:
            conn.execute("ROLLBACK")
        except sqlite3.Error:
            pass
        raise

def append_line(store, line):
    """One-row transaction. Failure is silent (rc stays 0): today's appends all ended
    in `>> "$f" 2>/dev/null || true`, and a hook crashing on a full disk would violate
    its always-exit-0 contract."""
    conn = _connect(True)
    if conn is None:
        return
    if store == "status":               # a projection has no table to append to
        rewrite(store, lambda rows: rows + [line])
        return
    n = COLS[store]
    try:
        conn.execute("BEGIN IMMEDIATE")
        conn.execute("INSERT INTO %s (nf, %s, fx) VALUES (%s)"
                     % (store, _cols(store), ", ".join("?" * (n + 2))),
                     _to_row(line, n))
        conn.execute("COMMIT")
    except sqlite3.Error:
        try:
            conn.execute("ROLLBACK")
        except sqlite3.Error:
            pass


def now_stamp():
    return time.strftime("%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S")   # `date '+%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S'`

def canon(path):
    """Best-effort `cd <path> && pwd -P`. For a path that exists this is exactly the
    board's canonical form (physical, symlinks resolved); for one that does not we
    still return a resolved string so raw-string callers can compare it (close keeps
    the string as given when cd fails)."""
    return os.path.realpath(path)

def canon_or_raw(path):
    """_ccres_keys' rule: canonicalize when the dir can be entered, otherwise keep
    the string VERBATIM (not prefix-resolved) — a dead row only ever matches a
    caller that named its exact string."""
    return os.path.realpath(path) if os.path.isdir(path) else path

def dir_match(row_dir, given, given_canon):
    """The ONE row-matching rule (file header, "Dir matching"): raw-string equality
    or canonical equality. Every verb takes both forms of the caller's dir, so a
    legacy logical-path row and a canonical caller (or the reverse) still find each
    other — the /var-vs-/private/var row that used to be unmatched forever."""
    return row_dir == given or canon_or_raw(row_dir) == given_canon

def under_root(row_dir, root_canon):
    """Is this row's dir inside <root>? Uses canon() (realpath), NOT canon_or_raw:
    an archived row's worktree is usually already GONE, and realpath still resolves
    a dead /var/... path to its /private/var/... form because the symlink component
    is what moves. Component-boundary test, never a bare prefix — a bare startswith
    would put /a/repo-other inside /a/repo."""
    if not row_dir:
        return False                       # an empty dir belongs to no repo: never touch it
    c = canon(row_dir)
    return c == root_canon or c.startswith(root_canon + os.sep)

def fields(line):
    return line.split("\t")

def _sanitize(text, maxlen, pipes_to_slash):
    """The retired cc-board.sh log's cleaning (the writer these bytes were frozen
    from — test.sh §32's oracle, Task 9), byte for byte: TAB and newline collapse to
    one space each, task summaries additionally trade | for / (keeps TSV and the
    `column` board intact), then the cut. largs gets no pipe translation. Truncates
    by CHARACTER (see the deviations note in the header)."""
    s = text.replace("\t", " ").replace("\n", " ")
    if pipes_to_slash:
        s = s.replace("|", "/")
    return s[:maxlen]

def _sanitize_field(text):
    """The corruption guard for fields with no length contract (e.g. the sidecar's
    state): collapse TAB/newline so a stray one cannot split the row, and nothing
    else — no truncation, no pipe translation."""
    return text.replace("\t", " ").replace("\n", " ")

def _git_branch(dir_):
    """`git -C <dir> rev-parse --abbrev-ref HEAD`, '?' when it fails (the retired
    log's rule) —
    but a CLEAN '?' (see the deviations note: the shell capture can splice a
    printed "HEAD" onto the fallback). rc 0 with empty output writes an EMPTY
    field — replicated, not second-guessed."""
    try:
        r = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", dir_, "rev-parse", "--abbrev-ref", "HEAD"],
                           capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=10)
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError):
        return "?"
    if r.returncode != 0:
        return "?"
    return r.stdout.strip()

# The tab-opened dedup timestamp is a COLUMN now (spec §3.1). It replaced a marker FILE
# under $TMPDIR whose name was the sha1 of the dir, and that path carried a defect that
# dies with it: when `shasum` was unavailable the hash came back EMPTY, so the marker
# path resolved to the containing DIRECTORY — `[ -e ]` then answered true forever and
# every dispatch, for every dir, was silently swallowed for 120s. An integer column
# cannot have an empty key. (The literal path is deliberately NOT written here: a grep
# for it is the assertion that the marker never comes back. Story: docs/known-issues.md.)
def _task_seq(conn, dir_):
    """seq of the NEWEST tasks row for <dir>, or None. dir_match, like every verb."""
    c = canon(dir_)
    hit = None
    for seq, d in conn.execute("SELECT seq, dir FROM tasks ORDER BY seq"):
        if dir_match("" if d is None else _dec(d), dir_, c):
            hit = seq
    return hit

def _usage(text):
    print("usage: cc-state " + text, file=sys.stderr)
    return 2

# ── tasks store ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Row: time \t branch \t surface-ref \t dir \t caller \t task \t parent \t launch-args
# (7-field legacy rows are legal and must round-trip as 7 fields).

def cmd_task_add(args):
    """The dispatch-time placeholder row — the retired cc-board.sh log's row (its
    recorded bytes are test.sh §32's frozen oracle, Task 9; including the '?'
    fallbacks for an empty surface ref and an underivable branch) with caller
    and launch-args left empty for task-set-launch. The dir is canonicalized on the
    way in (the file header's write-side rule). Does NOT stamp the dedup timestamp:
    it says "a tab actually opened", which only the caller knows — see
    task-mark-opened.

    It REPLACES the dir's row rather than appending a second one (§3.1: "dir 做主键
    就吃掉了 newest-per-dir 去重"). The replace is here, in the verb, rather than a
    UNIQUE index on the column, for two measured reasons: the index would key on the
    raw string, and the rule is dir_match — a legacy row holding the logical /var
    form is the same worktree as the canonical one this row names, and the index
    would happily keep both; and it would take `load`'s deliberate "no dedup, raw
    bytes" contract with it, which is the path a human repairs a store by. So the
    invariant is enforced at every write the PRODUCT makes and `load` stays the
    documented footgun; task-prune --compact still collapses what `load` and the
    legacy import can still put there. docs/state-model-d-plan.md §8.

    The two columns that live outside the line follow the DIR, not the row: _put
    snapshots them before the rewrite and re-attaches them by dir_match after, so
    a re-dispatch keeps the worktree's agent state and its tab-opened stamp — which
    is what they did when they were a sidecar file and a $TMPDIR marker keyed by dir."""
    if len(args) != 5:
        return _usage("task-add <dir> <branch> <ref> <task> <parent>")
    d, branch, ref, task, parent = args
    if not d:
        return 0                              # the retired log: [ -n "$dir" ] || exit 0
    d = canon(d)
    if not branch:
        branch = _git_branch(d)               # empty arg = derive here, as the retired log did
    ref = ref or "?"                          # the retired log: ref="${2:-?}"
    summary = _sanitize(task, 140, True) or "(idle ccteam, no initial prompt)"
    fresh = "\t".join([now_stamp(), branch, ref, d, "", summary, parent, ""])
    # d is already canonical, so both halves of dir_match get the same string; what the
    # second half still buys is the LEGACY row whose dir is the logical /var form.
    def fn(rows):
        return [l for l in rows
                if not (len(fields(l)) > TASK.dir
                        and dir_match(fields(l)[TASK.dir], d, d))] + [fresh]
    rewrite("tasks", fn)                  # failure stays silent: today's append ended in
    return 0                              # `>> "$f" 2>/dev/null || true`

def cmd_task_set_launch(args):
    """Fill caller-ref (field 5) and launch-args (field 8) on the NEWEST row of the
    dir — the second half of the dispatch that task-add started. No row → silent
    no-op rc 0 (mirrors task-set-state: nothing to complete)."""
    if len(args) != 3:
        return _usage("task-set-launch <dir> <caller-ref> <launch-args>")
    d, caller, largs = args
    c = canon(d)
    def fn(rows):
        out = list(rows)                       # build NEW rows — fn must never
        for i in range(len(out) - 1, -1, -1):  # mutate in place, or rewrite()'s
            f = fields(out[i])                 # out != rows change check compares
            if len(f) > TASK.dir and dir_match(f[TASK.dir], d, c):   # an object against itself
                f[TASK.caller_ref] = caller   # written raw — exactly the retired log's $caller
                if len(f) > TASK.launch_args:
                    f[TASK.launch_args] = _sanitize(largs, 200, False)
                out[i] = "\t".join(f)
                break
        return out
    rewrite("tasks", fn)
    return 0

def cmd_task_get(args):
    """The newest row whose dir field matches <dir> under the file-header rule.
    rc 1 + no output when absent."""
    if len(args) != 1:
        return _usage("task-get <dir>")
    c = canon(args[0])
    for line in reversed(read_lines("tasks")):
        f = fields(line)
        if len(f) > TASK.dir and dir_match(f[TASK.dir], args[0], c):
            print(line)
            return 0
    return 1

def cmd_task_list(args):
    """newest-per-dir + repo filter + dead-dir skip in one pass — what the board
    render and gwt-resume ask for. Dedup keys on the CANONICAL dir (the render's
    SEEN[c], which subsumes the raw key since production rows are written
    canonical); dead dirs are skipped AT READ TIME (resume's `[ -d ]` rule — the
    file-level sweep is task-prune's job); output is newest-first (tail -r order).
    --all disables the repo filter, in either flag order (cc-board.sh's contract).
    --archive instead reads the archive: a history — every row, file order, no
    dead-dir skip (its dirs are gone by design).

    --with-state appends the sidecar's state and its epoch as two extra fields ('-'
    and '' when there is none). The join belongs HERE, not in the caller: it keys on
    dir_match, so a legacy logical /var row still finds the canonical /private/var
    key the hook writes (spec 3.5 defect 3 — the board used to get this right only
    by re-canonicalizing every dir on READ, which is the half of defect 3 we are
    removing). A caller doing the join itself keys on the raw string and silently
    shows '-' for exactly those rows. It also keeps the API question-oriented
    (design 2, conclusion 3): one call answers "the board's rows and their state"
    instead of the caller making a second one per store."""
    repo_val, archive, all_flag, with_state = None, False, False, False
    i = 0
    while i < len(args):
        a = args[i]
        if a == "--all":
            all_flag = True
        elif a == "--archive":
            archive = True
        elif a == "--with-state":
            with_state = True
        elif a == "--repo" and i + 1 < len(args):
            repo_val = args[i + 1]; i += 1
        else:
            return _usage("task-list [--all] [--repo <root>] [--archive] [--with-state]")
        i += 1
    state_rows = []
    if with_state:
        state_rows = [fields(l) for l in read_lines("status")]

    def decorate(line, row_dir, row_canon):
        if not with_state:
            return line
        for sf in state_rows:
            if len(sf) > SIDE.state and dir_match(sf[SIDE.dir], row_dir, row_canon):
                return (line + "\t" + sf[SIDE.state] + "\t"
                        + (sf[SIDE.ts] if len(sf) > SIDE.ts else ""))
        return line + "\t-\t"
    repo = None if all_flag or repo_val is None else canon(repo_val)
    def under(c):
        return not repo or c == repo or c.startswith(repo + "/")
    if archive:
        for line in read_lines("archive"):
            f = fields(line)
            if len(f) > TASK.dir and under(canon_or_raw(f[TASK.dir])):
                print(decorate(line, f[TASK.dir], canon(f[TASK.dir])))
        return 0
    seen = set()
    for line in reversed(read_lines("tasks")):
        f = fields(line)
        if len(f) <= TASK.dir or not f[TASK.dir] or not os.path.isdir(f[TASK.dir]):
            continue
        c = canon(f[TASK.dir])
        if c in seen:
            continue
        seen.add(c)
        if under(c):
            print(decorate(line, f[TASK.dir], c))
    return 0

def cmd_task_set_state(args):
    """The hook's sidecar write. Membership follows the file-header dir rule (an
    unknown dir is a silent no-op that writes not one byte), the state value is
    corruption-guarded (a TAB in it must not split the sidecar row), and `ready` is
    refused outright: readiness stays owned by gwt-done + a clean tree (spec §8
    invariant 3), and this verb is the one place that rule can be structurally
    enforced."""
    if len(args) != 2:
        return _usage("task-set-state <dir> <state>")
    d, state = args
    if state == "ready":
        print("cc-state: the sidecar never records ready — readiness is gwt-done + "
              "a clean tree, never a state cell", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    state = _sanitize_field(state)
    c = canon(d)
    if not any(len(f) > TASK.dir and dir_match(f[TASK.dir], d, c)
               for f in (fields(l) for l in read_lines("tasks"))):
        return 0
    fresh = "\t".join([c, state, str(int(time.time()))])
    def fn(rows):
        return [l for l in rows
                if not (len(fields(l)) > SIDE.dir
                        and dir_match(fields(l)[SIDE.dir], d, c))] + [fresh]
    rewrite("status", fn)
    return 0

def cmd_task_clear_state(args):
    """Drop a dir's sidecar rows. The counterpart of task-set-state, and the verb
    resume and gwt-rm need: BOTH clear the agent-state row of a dir that is still
    ALIVE, which no other verb does — task-prune only sweeps dirs that are gone and
    task-drop only touches the task list. Deliberately NOT gated on board
    membership the way task-set-state is: every caller clears precisely when the
    task row is about to go away or has already gone, so requiring one would make
    the verb a no-op exactly when it is needed. No matching row is a silent rc 0."""
    if len(args) != 1:
        return _usage("task-clear-state <dir>")
    d = args[0]
    c = canon(d)
    def fn(rows):
        return [l for l in rows
                if not (len(fields(l)) > SIDE.dir
                        and dir_match(fields(l)[SIDE.dir], d, c))]
    rewrite("status", fn)
    return 0

def _swap_suuid(la, u):
    """_ccres_setref's setsuuid: replace the suuid= segment of a launch-args field,
    keeping model= LAST (a model id may itself contain colons — every parser stops
    there). Byte-equivalent to the awk, one-colon strip included."""
    mod, pre = "", la
    j = la.find("model=")
    if j != -1:
        mod, pre = la[j:], la[:j]
        if pre.endswith(":"):
            pre = pre[:-1]
    segs = [s for s in pre.split(":") if s != "" and not s.startswith("suuid=")]
    segs.append("suuid=" + u)
    return ":".join(segs) + ((":" + mod) if mod else "")

def cmd_task_set_ref(args):
    """Resume's refresh: field 3 (surface ref) on EVERY row of the dir (the
    file-header dir rule), plus the recorded suuid when given. Rows without a
    launch-args field keep their field count: the $3=r OFS rebuild that expanded
    7-field rows (spec §3.5 defect 6) is deliberately NOT replicated."""
    if len(args) not in (2, 3):
        return _usage("task-set-ref <dir> <ref> [<suuid>]")
    d, ref = args[0], args[1]
    su = args[2] if len(args) == 3 else ""
    c = canon(d)
    def fn(rows):
        out = list(rows)                       # new list, not in-place (see
        for i, line in enumerate(out):         # task-set-launch's note)
            f = fields(line)
            if len(f) > TASK.dir and dir_match(f[TASK.dir], d, c):
                f[TASK.surface_ref] = ref
                if su and len(f) > TASK.launch_args:
                    f[TASK.launch_args] = _swap_suuid(f[TASK.launch_args], su)
                out[i] = "\t".join(f)
        return out
    rewrite("tasks", fn)
    return 0

def cmd_task_drop(args):
    """gwt-rm's row removal, under the file-header dir rule."""
    if len(args) != 1:
        return _usage("task-drop <dir>")
    d = args[0]
    c = canon(d)
    def fn(rows):
        return [l for l in rows
                if not (len(fields(l)) > TASK.dir
                        and dir_match(fields(l)[TASK.dir], d, c))]
    rewrite("tasks", fn)
    return 0

def cmd_task_prune(args):
    """Sweep rows whose dir is gone, from the task list AND the sidecar — both real
    callers (the board's prune-on-read and gwt-prune) always sweep the pair. Keep
    rule is today's: dir field non-empty and still a directory."""
    compact = False
    for a in args:
        if a == "--compact":
            compact = True
        else:
            return _usage("task-prune [--compact]")

    def live(rows, col):
        return [l for l in rows
                if len(fields(l)) > col and fields(l)[col]
                and os.path.isdir(fields(l)[col])]

    def newest_per_dir(rows):
        """gwt-prune's `tail -r | awk -F'\t' '$4 != "" && !seen[$4]++' | tail -r`,
        verbatim. Two things are load-bearing and deliberately NOT "improved":
        the key is the RAW dir string (today does not canonicalize here, so two
        forms of one dir are two rows — changing that is a behaviour change, not a
        cleanup), and the surviving rows keep their ORIGINAL file order."""
        seen, out = set(), []
        for l in reversed(rows):
            f = fields(l)
            d = f[TASK.dir] if len(f) > TASK.dir else ""
            if not d or d in seen:
                continue
            seen.add(d)
            out.append(l)
        out.reverse()
        return out

    ok = rewrite("tasks", lambda rows: newest_per_dir(live(rows, TASK.dir)) if compact
                 else live(rows, TASK.dir))
    if not ok:
        # today's gwt-prune on a failed compaction: the list is left as it was, loudly
        print("cc-state: compaction failed — task list left untouched", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    rewrite("status", lambda rows: live(rows, SIDE.dir))
    return 0

def cmd_task_archive(args):
    """Move every row of a branch into the archive (verbatim + merged-at appended,
    8→9 fields), sweep the sidecar rows of the moved dirs, print the moved dirs.
    With --repo <root> only rows whose dir lives under that root are moved, which
    is spec 3.5 defect 2 dying: _gwt_archive_branch matched on branch NAME alone,
    so merging feat/x in one repo archived another repo's feat/x rows too. Without
    the flag the semantics are today's, global — every existing caller and fixture
    keeps working unchanged.
    <merged-into> — "where this actually landed" — is appended after merged-at, and
    ONLY when there is something to record. That condition is the whole design: every
    caller before phase D passed "", so an empty one has to leave the row byte-identical
    to what the frozen archive fixtures pin, while a real one grows the row the same way
    merged-at already does (a 7-field legacy row archives to 8, then 9). It is the branch
    the merge LANDED ON, which gwt-merge's --into override and a fast-forwarded target
    both make different from the parent recorded at dispatch — and only this one is
    history. Sanitized like every other caller-supplied string: a TAB in it would forge a
    field. Under the tasks lock like today (the archive write borrows it), plus the archive's own
    lock — ordering is always tasks→archive, so no deadlock. A failed tasks rewrite
    is LOUD (stderr + rc 1), matching _gwt_archive_branch: the task list stays
    untouched and the archive may have gained the rows — visible, never silent."""
    root = None
    rest = []
    i = 0
    while i < len(args):
        if args[i] == "--repo":
            if i + 1 >= len(args):
                return _usage("task-archive <branch> <merged-into> [--repo <root>]")
            root = args[i + 1]; i += 2
        else:
            rest.append(args[i]); i += 1
    if len(rest) != 2:
        return _usage("task-archive <branch> <merged-into> [--repo <root>]")
    branch = rest[0]
    if not branch:
        return 0
    into = _sanitize_field(rest[1])
    root_canon = canon(root) if root else None
    ts = str(int(time.time()))
    conn = _connect(False)
    ok, moved_dirs = True, []
    if conn is None:
        # Two very different Nones. No store at all means there is nothing of this branch to
        # move — rc 0, today's behaviour. A store that EXISTS but cannot be opened (unwritable
        # library, corrupt file) is the failure the old unwritable-store-dir path used to
        # produce, and it has to stay LOUD: swallowing it would report "archived nothing" for
        # a branch whose rows are still sitting there.
        if _LAST_ERR is None:
            return 0
        ok = False
    try:
        if conn is None:
            raise sqlite3.Error("state library unavailable")
        conn.execute("BEGIN IMMEDIATE")
        lines = _get(conn, "tasks")          # inside the transaction (B1's rule)
        kept, moved_rows = [], []
        for line in lines:
            f = fields(line)
            hit = len(f) > TASK.branch and f[TASK.branch] == branch
            if hit and root_canon is not None:
                hit = under_root(f[TASK.dir] if len(f) > TASK.dir else "", root_canon)
            if hit:
                moved_rows.append(line + "\t" + ts + ("\t" + into if into else ""))
                if len(f) > TASK.dir and f[TASK.dir]:
                    moved_dirs.append(f[TASK.dir])
            else:
                kept.append(line)
        # The whole move is ONE transaction, which is what retires spec 3.5 defect 5: the
        # archive append and the task-list rewrite used to be two separate writes, so a
        # failure between them left rows in both places and the error message had to admit
        # it. The message below is kept verbatim anyway — it is user-visible text, its
        # defensive wording costs nothing, and the C2b report carries the reachability
        # analysis rather than a silent edit.
        # ONE exit, one COMMIT: "nothing matched" still has a transaction open and has to
        # close it, and writing that as a second COMMIT would make "this function opens
        # exactly one transaction" unassertable — the property §39 pins is structural, so
        # the structure has to stay legible.
        if moved_rows:
            _put(conn, "archive", _get(conn, "archive") + moved_rows)
            _put(conn, "tasks", kept)
        conn.execute("COMMIT")
        if not moved_rows:
            return 0
    except sqlite3.Error:
        if conn is not None:                  # may never have opened at all
            try:
                conn.execute("ROLLBACK")
            except sqlite3.Error:
                pass
        ok = False
    if not ok:
        print("cc-state: archive rewrite failed — task list left untouched "
              "(the archive may have gained rows)", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    moved_canon = [(d, canon(d)) for d in moved_dirs]
    def fn(rows):
        return [l for l in rows
                if not any(dir_match(fields(l)[SIDE.dir], d, c) for d, c in moved_canon)]
    rewrite("status", fn)
    for d in moved_dirs:
        print(d)
    return 0

def cmd_task_mark_opened(args):
    """Stamp the dedup marker — ONLY after the tab actually opened. task-add
    deliberately does not do this: a dispatch that recorded its board row but
    failed to open the tab must leave no marker, or the retry is silently eaten
    for 120s (cc-dispatch.sh: "failures leave no blocking marker")."""
    if len(args) != 1:
        return _usage("task-mark-opened <dir>")
    conn = _connect(True)
    if conn is None:
        return 0                       # `|| true` today; never fail a dispatch
    try:
        conn.execute("BEGIN IMMEDIATE")
        seq = _task_seq(conn, args[0])
        if seq is not None:
            conn.execute("UPDATE tasks SET tab_opened_ts=? WHERE seq=?",
                         (int(time.time()), seq))
        conn.execute("COMMIT")
    except sqlite3.Error:
        try:
            conn.execute("ROLLBACK")
        except sqlite3.Error:
            pass
    return 0

def cmd_task_opened_recently(args):
    """rc 0 = a tab was opened for this dir within <seconds> (the old 120s marker
    question). Read-only; task-mark-opened is the writer."""
    if len(args) != 2:
        return _usage("task-opened-recently <dir> <seconds>")
    try:
        win = int(args[1])
    except ValueError:
        return _usage("task-opened-recently <dir> <seconds>")
    conn = _connect(False)             # a read must not bring the library into existence
    if conn is None:
        return 1
    try:
        seq = _task_seq(conn, args[0])
        if seq is None:
            return 1
        row = conn.execute("SELECT tab_opened_ts FROM tasks WHERE seq=?",
                           (seq,)).fetchone()
    except sqlite3.Error:
        return 1
    # tab_opened_ts ONLY, never created_at (spec §3.1, window B): a row re-opened by
    # resume keeps its original created_at, so reading that would let the dispatch right
    # after a resume through the dedup gate and open a SECOND tab for the same dir.
    if not row or row[0] is None:
        return 1
    return 0 if (time.time() - row[0]) < win else 1

# ── tabs store ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Row: suuid \t owner \t dir \t session \t time. Empty owner/dir/session are written
# as "-" ON PURPOSE — `read` collapses runs of TABs, so an empty middle field would
# shift every later field for the reader. The two ledgers answer different
# questions ("whose sub-task is this" vs "who opened this tab") and are NEVER
# deduped against each other; `close` consults them in that order.
_UUID_CHARS = set("0123456789ABCDEF-")

def _valid_uuid(s):
    return bool(s) and all(c in _UUID_CHARS for c in s)

def _self_uuid():
    # the caller's stable surface uuid, uppercased like every ledger comparison
    return (os.environ.get("CC_CALLER_SURFACE_UUID")
            or os.environ.get("CMUX_SURFACE_ID") or "").upper()

def _largs_seg(la, key):
    """_ccpick: the <key>= segment of a launch-args field. model= is composed LAST
    and may itself contain colons, so every parser stops at it."""
    for seg in la.split("model=", 1)[0].split(":"):
        if seg.startswith(key + "="):
            return seg[len(key) + 1:]
    return ""

def _identity(s):
    """close's treatment of a recorded identity (B3): uppercase it, and anything
    that is not a plain uuid is not an identity at all — a short ref is an address,
    never an identity, and must never crowd out a real candidate."""
    s = s.upper()
    return s if _valid_uuid(s) else ""

def cmd_tab_add(args):
    """_cctabs_log: only a plain uuid is ever a suuid (a short ref is an address,
    not an identity — it drifts); anything else is silently NOT recorded, rc 0.
    An owner that is not a plain uuid is an UNKNOWN owner (written "-"), never a
    broken row. dir/session get today's TAB/newline collapse; empty → "-"."""
    if len(args) != 4:
        return _usage("tab-add <suuid> <owner> <dir> <session-id>")
    su, owner, d, sid = args
    su = su.upper()
    if not _valid_uuid(su):
        return 0
    owner = owner.upper()
    if not _valid_uuid(owner):
        owner = ""
    d = d.replace("\t", " ").replace("\n", " ") or "-"
    sid = sid.replace("\t", " ").replace("\n", " ") or "-"
    append_line("tabs", "\t".join([su, owner or "-", d, sid, now_stamp()]))
    return 0

def cmd_tab_list(args):
    """The ledger read behind `cc-dispatch.sh tabs`: raw rows, minus (without
    --all) the rows another session opened. A truncated row (a killed writer, a
    hand edit) is skipped or shown, never a traceback — awk never crashed here.
    No session identity at all = no filter, same as the shell loop. The cmux
    liveness join stays in the caller: it is a probe, not state."""
    if args not in ([], ["--all"]):
        return _usage("tab-list [--all]")
    self_u = _self_uuid()
    for line in read_lines("tabs"):
        f = fields(line)
        if not f[TAB.suuid]:
            continue
        owner = ("" if len(f) <= TAB.owner or f[TAB.owner] == "-" else f[TAB.owner])
        if args == [] and self_u and owner != self_u:
            continue
        print(line)
    return 0

def _tabs_owner_of(suuid):
    """_cctabs_owner: the NEWEST ledger row for the uuid that actually names an
    owner ("-" and empty do not count)."""
    for line in reversed(read_lines("tabs")):
        f = fields(line)
        if len(f) > TAB.owner and f[TAB.suuid] == suuid and f[TAB.owner] not in ("", "-"):
            return f[TAB.owner]
    return ""

def cmd_tab_resolve(args):
    """Every ledger candidate for a dir, in the priority `close` consults them:
    one line per candidate, `source \\t suuid \\t owner \\t branch` (source =
    board | tabs), `-` placeholders for empty owner/branch. Identities get close's
    own treatment (uppercase; a non-uuid is dropped outright) — a recorded short
    ref must never masquerade as an identity or crowd out the real tabs candidate.
    Each candidate carries the owner close would compute HAD it chosen that
    candidate: the board row's csuuid falling back to the ledger's newest
    by-uuid owner; the tabs candidate's owner is that by-uuid lookup (close's
    by-dir owner is dead code — computed, never read). NB: when a board row's
    suuid is not a valid uuid NO board candidate is emitted, and with it that
    row's branch is absent from the output too (the tabs candidate carries "-") —
    harmless to close's decision, but a caller mining branch for display text
    gets nothing for that row. The facade does NOT choose:
    liveness probing and the cascade belong to the caller (spec §4), and the cmux
    session store never enters here. Nothing resolvable → no output, rc 1."""
    if len(args) != 1:
        return _usage("tab-resolve <dir>")
    raw_given = args[0]
    canon_given = canon(raw_given)
    out = []
    for line in reversed(read_lines("tasks")):   # newest board row for the dir
        f = fields(line)
        if (len(f) > TASK.dir and f[TASK.dir]
                and dir_match(f[TASK.dir], raw_given, canon_given)):
            su, owner = "", ""
            if len(f) > TASK.launch_args:
                su = _identity(_largs_seg(f[TASK.launch_args], "suuid"))
                owner = _largs_seg(f[TASK.launch_args], "csuuid").upper()
            if su:
                if not owner:
                    owner = _tabs_owner_of(su)
                out.append("\t".join(
                    ["board", su, owner or "-",
                     (f[TASK.branch] if len(f) > TASK.branch and f[TASK.branch] else "-")]))
            break                               # close reads the newest row only
    for line in reversed(read_lines("tabs")):    # _cctabs_by_dir: RAW row dir ==
        f = fields(line)                         # canon(given) or the given string
        if (len(f) > TAB.dir and f[TAB.suuid]
                and dir_match(f[TAB.dir], raw_given, canon_given)):
            su = _identity(f[TAB.suuid])
            if su:
                out.append("\t".join(
                    ["tabs", su, _tabs_owner_of(su) or "-", "-"]))
            break                               # close reads the newest row only
    if not out:
        return 1
    for line in out:
        print(line)
    return 0

def cmd_tab_owner(args):
    if len(args) != 1:
        return _usage("tab-owner <suuid>")
    o = _tabs_owner_of(args[0].upper())
    if not o:
        return 1
    print(o)
    return 0

def cmd_tab_prune(args):
    """The !partial invariant, kept STRUCTURAL (spec §8 invariant 4): the argument
    is the RAW live map — _cctabs_livemap's output, `<short-ref>\\t<uuid>[\\t<ws>]`
    per live surface plus a final `!partial` sentinel whenever the enumeration was
    incomplete — and the sentinel is recognized HERE. A caller that pre-extracts a
    flat uuid list has already thrown the completeness bit away and would hand us
    "complete-looking" evidence for a partial probe; pruning off it deletes the
    ledger rows of every tab in an unenumerated workspace (the 2026-08-16
    incident, unrecoverable). Partial map, empty map, missing map, or a map that
    yields no keys at all → rc 0, not one row touched. Complete evidence keeps
    exactly the rows whose suuid is live (keys from field 2, uppercased like the
    map's own toupper($2)); an emptied ledger deletes its file, as every rewriter
    here does."""
    if len(args) != 1:
        return _usage("tab-prune <live-map-file>")
    try:
        with _open_rd(args[0]) as f:
            map_lines = f.read().split("\n")
    except OSError:
        return 0
    if any(l.strip() == "!partial" for l in map_lines):
        return 0
    keys = set()
    for l in map_lines:                   # cmux's live map, NOT a store: its own format
        mf = l.split("\t")               # (surface-ref \t uuid \t workspace). Named `mf`
        if len(mf) >= 2 and mf[1]:        # so the "no bare position on a store row" pin
            keys.add(mf[1].upper())       # in §39 does not have to carve out an exception
    if not keys:
        return 0
    def fn(rows):
        return [l for l in rows
                if len(fields(l)) > TAB.owner and fields(l)[TAB.suuid] in keys]
    rewrite("tabs", fn)
    return 0

# ── verbs ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
def cmd_dump(args):
    if len(args) != 1 or args[0] not in PATHS:
        print("usage: cc-state dump tasks|status|archive|tabs", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    # This used to be a raw byte passthrough of the store FILE, with a comment saying
    # "never reconstruct from parsed fields". There is no file any more, so dump can
    # ONLY reconstruct — byte equivalence stops being a passthrough guarantee and
    # becomes a REBUILD guarantee, paid for by nf (a 7-field legacy row renders as 7
    # fields) and seq (rows come back in insertion order). §32's frozen oracle is what
    # keeps that honest: F32OR1/F32OR2 are literals recorded from the writer this
    # facade replaced, so they compare the rebuild against something independent of it.
    # ONE deliberate deviation lands here, written up in state-model §3.5: a hand-edited
    # final line with no trailing newline gets one on the way through.
    #
    # An empty store is silent rc 0 (today's behaviour for a missing file) and a
    # vanished downstream reader is not an error either — but a library that cannot be
    # opened or read IS loud, like cat's non-zero exit. Swallowing that with a blanket
    # `except OSError` (BrokenPipeError is an OSError!) would report partial output as
    # success (round-3 gate C3), so the two are separated by hand.
    lines = read_lines(args[0])
    if _LAST_ERR is not None:
        print("cc-state: dump %s: %s" % (args[0], _LAST_ERR), file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    try:
        if lines:
            sys.stdout.buffer.write(_enc("\n".join(lines) + "\n"))
    except BrokenPipeError:
        pass
    except OSError as e:
        print("cc-state: dump %s: %s" % (args[0], e), file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    return 0

def cmd_load(args):
    """`dump`'s inverse: REPLACE a store's rows with the raw lines of <file> ('-' =
    stdin). rc 0 = written, 1 = the source could not be read (or the write failed),
    2 = usage.

    Why this exists. Until the stores were files, "fix one corrupted row" was
    `$EDITOR worktree-tasks.tsv` — the move this project actually made all through
    audit-0821. `dump` alone gives that back only as far as READING; the pair
    `dump > f && $EDITOR f && load f` gives back the whole move. The same property
    is what the suite needs from a fixture: 7-field legacy rows, an empty middle
    field, a lone non-UTF-8 byte are expressible as BYTES and in no other way, so
    the fixtures that used to `printf` straight at the file come through here.

    It deliberately bypasses every verb's semantics — no canonicalization, no
    sanitization, no membership rule, no dedup — and replaces the whole store
    rather than merging into it. That is a footgun, and it is exactly the footgun a
    text editor pointed at the TSV always was, so it gets documentation (--help,
    README) rather than a gate.

    One normalization, the same one the row reader already applies: the source is
    split on \\n and a missing final newline is supplied on write, so a hand-edited
    file whose last line lacks one still round-trips as that many rows. Bytes are
    read RAW and decoded with surrogateescape — a store holding a non-UTF-8 byte
    must survive dump → load → dump unchanged, and a locale-dependent decode (the
    text-mode default under LC_ALL=C) would corrupt it."""
    if len(args) != 2 or args[0] not in PATHS:
        return _usage("load tasks|status|archive|tabs <file>   "
                      "(- = stdin; REPLACES the whole store)")
    store, src = args
    try:
        if src == "-":
            raw = sys.stdin.buffer.read()
        else:
            with open(src, "rb") as f:
                raw = f.read()
    except OSError as e:
        print("cc-state: load %s: %s" % (store, e), file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    lines = raw.decode("utf-8", "surrogateescape").split("\n")
    if lines and lines[-1] == "":
        lines.pop()
    # C2a had to reach past the storage layer here (lock + _write_unlocked) to keep the
    # five storage functions byte-frozen for that task's gate. The engine swap folds it
    # back in, alongside task-archive's identical bypass: write_lines now returns success,
    # so the one verb whose whole job IS the write can say the write did not happen.
    if not write_lines(store, lines):
        print("cc-state: load %s: write failed, store left as it was" % store,
              file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    return 0

def cmd_exists(args):
    """rc 0 = this store holds at least one row, rc 1 = it does not. Zero output on
    both — the callers are `||` guards in shell, and a guard that prints is a guard
    that leaks into a board's output.

    The callers this replaces all asked `[ -f <store> ]`, and they asked it for one
    reason: to tell "there is no store" apart from "the store is there and nothing
    in it matched", which are two different sentences to a human. Those two
    questions were the same question only because the old rewriter DELETED a store it
    emptied, so a file that existed always had a row in it. Defining the verb on ROWS
    is what keeps the sentence the caller prints correct now that the four TSVs ARE
    one library and there is no per-store file left to stat. The contract did not move
    for the engine swap — only its implementation did, exactly as Task 1 predicted."""
    if len(args) != 1 or args[0] not in PATHS:
        return _usage("exists tasks|status|archive|tabs")
    return 0 if read_lines(args[0]) else 1

VERBS = {
    "dump": cmd_dump, "load": cmd_load, "exists": cmd_exists,
    "task-add": cmd_task_add, "task-set-launch": cmd_task_set_launch,
    "task-get": cmd_task_get, "task-list": cmd_task_list,
    "task-set-state": cmd_task_set_state, "task-clear-state": cmd_task_clear_state,
    "task-set-ref": cmd_task_set_ref,
    "task-drop": cmd_task_drop, "task-prune": cmd_task_prune,
    "task-archive": cmd_task_archive, "task-mark-opened": cmd_task_mark_opened,
    "task-opened-recently": cmd_task_opened_recently,
    "tab-add": cmd_tab_add, "tab-list": cmd_tab_list, "tab-resolve": cmd_tab_resolve,
    "tab-owner": cmd_tab_owner, "tab-prune": cmd_tab_prune,
}

def main(argv):
    if not argv or argv[0] in ("-h", "--help"):
        print("usage: cc-state <verb> ... — the single reader/writer of cc-stack state")
        print("verbs: " + " ".join(sorted(VERBS)))
        print("note: `load <store> <file|->` REPLACES that whole store, bypassing "
              "every verb's semantics — it is the write half of the hand-edit path "
              "in README's troubleshooting section.")
        return 2
    v = argv[0]
    if v not in VERBS:
        print("cc-state: unknown verb: " + v, file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    return VERBS[v](argv[1:])

def _shutdown():
    """Close the library before the interpreter goes away.

    Deterministic release of the file lock instead of whatever the interpreter does on
    the way out — which is not the same everywhere, and the difference is measured:
    3.14.7 (sqlite 3.53) ends up with `x.db`, 3.9.6 (macOS's system sqlite 3.51 — the
    build /usr/bin/python3 links, and therefore the one the HOOK runs on every prompt of
    every session) ends up with `x.db x.db-shm x.db-wal`.

    Note what this does NOT do: it does not make the two outcomes the same. The sidecars
    surviving a clean `close()` is the sqlite build's behaviour, not the interpreter's —
    measured directly, connect → write → close still leaves them on 3.51. They are
    harmless (the next open recovers them, sqlite owns their lifetime) and §39 asserts
    the honest invariant instead: a clean exit leaves nothing beside the library EXCEPT
    those two, and the next process sees every row.
    Never raises — this runs on the hook's path, where every failure must stay silent."""
    global _CONN
    if _CONN is None:
        return
    try:
        _CONN.close()
    except (sqlite3.Error, OSError):
        pass
    _CONN = None

if __name__ == "__main__":
    try:
        _rc = main(sys.argv[1:])
        sys.stdout.flush()   # surface EPIPE INSIDE the try: for a store whose
                             # writes all return cleanly before `head` exits, the
                             # broken pipe only shows up at the interpreter's
                             # exit-time flush — outside every handler, leaking a
                             # traceback onto stderr for ~90–136KB stores. Flushing
                             # here makes the handler below reachable at every
                             # size, both paths (round-3 gate C5).
        sys.exit(_rc)
    except BrokenPipeError:
        # The downstream reader (head) is gone: die quietly. Redirecting the fd to
        # devnull stops python's own exit-time flush from re-raising onto stderr
        # (85 bytes of traceback noise per `dump | head` on a big store — B13).
        os.dup2(os.open(os.devnull, os.O_WRONLY), sys.stdout.fileno())
        sys.exit(0)
    finally:
        # finally, not a call in each branch: an unhandled error is exactly when the
        # connection is most likely to be open, and SystemExit passes through here too.
        _shutdown()
